WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.920
What if the reason you can't get that image to stay put in your latex document

00:04.920 --> 00:09.020
isn't because the software is broken, but because you're actually attempting to

00:09.020 --> 00:12.920
defy the laws of physics? That is exactly the right question to ask.

00:13.040 --> 00:18.000
Right. Welcome to this custom-tailored deep dives designed specifically for you.

00:18.480 --> 00:23.320
If you're tuning in, you've likely experienced the, you know, the very specific profound

00:23.320 --> 00:28.700
frustration of trying to force a piece of text or a figure to just sit where you wanted to on the

00:28.700 --> 00:33.800
page. It's a universal experience for beginners. It really is. So we're pulling from a foundational

00:33.800 --> 00:40.240
source today, which is the Superooser Guide to Latex by Flyxion. And it basically argues that

00:40.240 --> 00:45.920
our entire mental model for document creation is totally flawed. Completely backward. Yeah. Yeah.

00:45.920 --> 00:51.100
Because we tend to treat latex as this, I mean, a glorified word processor, right? Like a really

00:51.100 --> 00:57.540
convoluted way to bold a header or render a calculus equation. But our mission today is to completely

00:57.540 --> 01:03.140
flip that narrative. And it requires a big shift. It does. We're going to explore how to treat a

01:03.140 --> 01:09.660
document, not as a static file, but as a fully programmable, reproducible publishing environment.

01:09.960 --> 01:15.280
We're going to learn how to engineer a book exactly the way a software engineer builds a massive,

01:15.540 --> 01:20.120
long-lived code base. Which is a beautiful way to work once you get it. Okay. Let's unpack this

01:20.120 --> 01:27.240
because moving from the sheer intimidation of a 500-page latex manual to actually discovering

01:27.240 --> 01:32.660
the matrix code behind beautifully typeset books requires a fundamental paradigm shift.

01:32.660 --> 01:37.600
It really requires abandoning the whole concept of a page as a blank canvas.

01:37.780 --> 01:37.900
Right.

01:38.080 --> 01:42.680
Because when you use a standard word processor, you're operating under this illusion of direct

01:42.680 --> 01:48.440
manipulation. You hit a key, a letter appears. You drag an image to a spot on the screen.

01:48.440 --> 01:49.660
And it feels intuitive.

01:49.980 --> 01:54.820
It feels intuitive, but it's entirely superficial. What Flyxion's Guide makes abundantly clear is

01:54.820 --> 02:00.120
that latex, or more accurately, the underlying text engine, it doesn't understand concepts like

02:00.120 --> 02:02.480
margins or paragraphs in the way a human does.

02:02.540 --> 02:03.840
Which is wild to think about.

02:03.900 --> 02:10.840
It really is. It operates on a strict, deterministic physics engine. The frustration most users feel,

02:11.020 --> 02:16.880
the command shattering, the figures floating away, having to compile twice all of that is actually

02:16.880 --> 02:20.100
predictable consequences of few underlying principles.

02:20.500 --> 02:21.780
Because they're fighting the physics engine.

02:21.980 --> 02:27.460
Exactly. They are fighting the physics rather than leveraging the constraints. Once you understand

02:27.460 --> 02:33.240
the fundamental primitives the compiler uses, that adversarial relationship just completely disappears.

02:33.460 --> 02:37.620
Okay. So to gain that control, we really have to start at the absolute atomic level. We need to

02:37.620 --> 02:38.840
look at the physics of the page.

02:38.920 --> 02:39.820
The raw materials.

02:39.820 --> 02:45.680
Yeah. The raw materials. The source material outlines that text models a typeset page using

02:45.680 --> 02:51.300
only three primitive materials. It doesn't see letters or spacing. It literally only sees boxes,

02:51.620 --> 02:52.580
glue, and penalties.

02:52.820 --> 02:53.640
That's all there is.

02:53.780 --> 02:58.800
And if you, the listener, don't internalize those three concepts, you will just forever be wrestling

02:58.800 --> 03:04.580
with the compiler. So let's break down the first one. Boxes. I mean, I always visualized a character

03:04.580 --> 03:07.160
just as a shape stamped in ink on a page.

03:07.160 --> 03:08.100
Right. Like a typewriter.

03:08.480 --> 03:12.020
Exactly. But the compiler actually requires a mathematical boundary.

03:12.240 --> 03:17.080
It does. The compiler models every single character as being enclosed in a rigid,

03:17.340 --> 03:19.260
two-dimensional rectangular box.

03:19.360 --> 03:19.560
Okay.

03:19.680 --> 03:24.720
And this isn't just a bounding box for rendering on your screen. It is a physical object within

03:24.720 --> 03:30.560
the engine's memory. Every character box has three very specific metric dimensions.

03:30.820 --> 03:32.040
Width, height, and depth.

03:32.040 --> 03:37.700
Yes. Width, height extending above the baseline and depth extending below the baseline to accommodate

03:37.700 --> 03:40.960
descenders. You know, the tails on letters like G, P, or Y.

03:41.020 --> 03:41.340
Right, right.

03:41.420 --> 03:46.800
But the crucial characteristic here is that these boxes are entirely rigid. They cannot be scaled.

03:46.980 --> 03:50.620
They can't be deformed. They can't intersect. They are solid matter.

03:50.960 --> 03:53.860
So the letter A is a box. The letter B is a box.

03:54.040 --> 03:58.980
Yes. And the composition of a whole document is simply the nesting of these boxes.

03:59.080 --> 03:59.820
Like Russian dolls.

03:59.820 --> 04:04.960
Essentially, yeah. A word is constructed by packing character boxes horizontally into a

04:04.960 --> 04:10.660
slightly larger box. A line of text is a larger horizontal box containing those word boxes.

04:10.780 --> 04:11.480
Okay, I follow.

04:11.700 --> 04:16.340
And then a paragraph is a vertical stack of those line boxes, which forms an even larger

04:16.340 --> 04:22.280
vertical box. And the page itself is just the ultimate vertical box. It's a strict hierarchical

04:22.280 --> 04:23.960
structure of rigid containers.

04:24.120 --> 04:28.320
That makes perfect sense for the solid matter. But, I mean, if everything is perfectly rigid,

04:28.320 --> 04:31.580
how does the text stretch to fill a page dynamically?

04:31.780 --> 04:32.380
It couldn't.

04:32.740 --> 04:38.080
Right, because if you only had rigid boxes, text justification would be mathematically impossible.

04:38.460 --> 04:43.020
You could never adjust the spacing to make the left and right edges perfectly flush.

04:43.280 --> 04:46.300
Which brings us to the second primitive, the glue.

04:46.500 --> 04:48.100
The glue. Yeah, let's talk about the glue.

04:48.380 --> 04:51.160
Glue is how text represents flexible space.

04:51.160 --> 04:56.380
A novice usually assumes that the space between words is just an empty character box.

04:56.620 --> 04:58.060
Just a blank, rigid box.

04:58.180 --> 05:01.160
Exactly. But it's not. It is literally a glob of glue.

05:01.460 --> 05:06.300
Glue has a natural, ideal width, usually the standard size of a space character in whatever

05:06.300 --> 05:07.100
font you're using.

05:07.220 --> 05:07.440
Okay.

05:07.440 --> 05:12.900
But crucially, it also has two dynamic properties, stretchability and shrinkability.

05:13.360 --> 05:13.840
Oh, interesting.

05:13.980 --> 05:20.340
Yeah, the engine can stretch the glue out or it can compress it down by algorithmically specified

05:20.340 --> 05:23.880
amounts just to accommodate all those rigid boxes we talked about.

05:24.120 --> 05:28.980
So the analogy that came to mind for me when I was reviewing this section of the source is

05:28.980 --> 05:30.900
physical masonry.

05:31.520 --> 05:32.840
Oh, that's a good way to look at it.

05:32.840 --> 05:38.600
Right. If you're building the brick wall, the bricks are your boxes, the words, the letters,

05:38.740 --> 05:41.800
the images. You can't compress a brick. It has fixed dimensions.

05:41.900 --> 05:42.500
The solid matter.

05:42.680 --> 05:48.460
Solid matter. But the wet mortar between the bricks, that is your glue. You can lay the

05:48.460 --> 05:52.820
water a little thicker or you can squish it a little thinner to ensure that the entire

05:52.820 --> 05:56.580
row of bricks perfectly hits the structural edges of the wall.

05:56.780 --> 05:58.720
That is an excellent mechanical analogy.

05:59.000 --> 05:59.340
Thanks.

05:59.340 --> 06:05.020
Because when the engine justifies a line of text, it basically evaluates the total natural

06:05.020 --> 06:07.900
width of the word boxes and the natural width of the glue.

06:08.560 --> 06:13.500
If the line is short of the right margin, it instructs the glue between the words to stretch.

06:13.740 --> 06:16.760
If it's slightly too long, it tells the glue to compress.

06:16.940 --> 06:18.100
And this works vertically too, right?

06:18.380 --> 06:23.880
Absolutely. The space between paragraphs, the padding above a section heading, the clearance

06:23.880 --> 06:27.240
around a math equation, that is all vertical glue.

06:27.440 --> 06:27.680
Wow.

06:27.680 --> 06:32.220
It dynamically absorbs all the variations in your content. So you don't have to sit there

06:32.220 --> 06:36.760
manually inserting carriage returns or tweaking line heights like in Word.

06:36.960 --> 06:40.540
But there have to be physical limits, right? I mean, eventually you run out of mortar. If you

06:40.540 --> 06:45.580
have a line with only three massive words, the glue between them can't stretch infinitely

06:45.580 --> 06:48.160
without violating the aesthetic rules of typography.

06:48.160 --> 06:55.120
Exactly. When the glue is forced to stretch beyond its defined tolerance, the engine registers a failure state.

06:55.380 --> 06:57.020
Oh, is this the famous error message?

06:57.180 --> 07:04.220
Yes. This is when you see the infamous underfull H-box or overfull H-box warnings in your compiler log.

07:04.280 --> 07:06.740
I've seen that so many times and never knew what it meant.

07:06.740 --> 07:11.100
It just means the system has run out of flexible space. It's being forced to violate its own

07:11.100 --> 07:16.080
typographic parameters. The text might spill into the margin because the glue refused to

07:16.080 --> 07:20.660
compress any further, or it might look incredibly gappy because the glue was stretched to the

07:20.660 --> 07:21.380
breaking point.

07:21.800 --> 07:26.380
Which introduces the third primitive, right? The mechanism the engine actually uses to make

07:26.380 --> 07:29.320
decisions about where to break those lines before the glue fails.

07:29.500 --> 07:29.900
Penalties.

07:30.160 --> 07:30.900
Penalties, yes.

07:30.960 --> 07:31.080
Yeah.

07:31.080 --> 07:33.920
They are the economic currency of the text engine.

07:34.300 --> 07:36.540
The economic currency. I like that.

07:36.820 --> 07:41.480
They're basically integer values attached to potential breaking points, either in a horizontal

07:41.480 --> 07:48.600
line or a vertical page that express a typographic preference without imposing a strict unbreakable

07:48.600 --> 07:48.880
rule.

07:49.060 --> 07:50.220
Okay. So it's a cost.

07:50.660 --> 07:51.960
It is essentially a cost.

07:52.200 --> 07:52.380
Yeah.

07:52.460 --> 07:56.820
A large positive penalty attached to a space between two words tells the engine,

07:56.820 --> 08:01.480
hey, it is very expensive to break the line here. Avoid it if possible.

08:01.540 --> 08:02.660
And a negative penalty.

08:03.100 --> 08:07.940
A large negative penalty acts as an incentive, actively encouraging a break.

08:08.080 --> 08:12.440
So this is how the engine handles widow and orphan control, right? Like if you have a single

08:12.440 --> 08:16.880
line of a paragraph threatening to spill onto the top of the next page, the engine just applies

08:16.880 --> 08:21.280
a massive positive penalty to that specific break point to deter the behavior.

08:21.380 --> 08:26.000
That's exactly how it works. And if I recall, the source material notes that a penalty of negative

08:26.000 --> 08:29.820
10,000 is the mathematical equivalent of forcing a break outright.

08:30.320 --> 08:35.800
Yes. And positive 10,000 forbids it entirely. It's an absolute ban. And to really see the

08:35.800 --> 08:40.740
brilliance of this physics engine, you have to look at how it actually processes a paragraph

08:40.740 --> 08:43.820
using the Nuthplass line breaking algorithm.

08:44.220 --> 08:49.200
Let's really dig into this algorithm because a lot of standard software development relies on greedy

08:49.200 --> 08:49.860
algorithms.

08:50.100 --> 08:51.380
Right. Which are very different.

08:51.380 --> 08:55.660
Yeah. A greedy algorithm for line breaking, which is what older word processors used,

08:56.080 --> 09:01.180
just grabs as many word boxes as it can, crams them into the first line until it hits the margin,

09:01.600 --> 09:04.460
breaks the line, and then starts over on line two.

09:04.900 --> 09:09.440
And a greedy algorithm is inherently short-sighted. It optimizes locally.

09:09.760 --> 09:11.360
Because it doesn't care about the rest of the paragraph.

09:11.520 --> 09:16.780
Exactly. You might get a perfectly tight, aesthetically pleasing first line, but by the time you reach the

09:16.780 --> 09:24.020
fourth line, you're left with one massive word and no flexible glue, resulting in a total typographic

09:24.020 --> 09:30.360
disaster. The Nuthplass algorithm operates differently. It utilizes dynamic programming to

09:30.360 --> 09:33.340
evaluate the entire paragraph simultaneously.

09:33.560 --> 09:34.500
The whole thing at once.

09:34.500 --> 09:41.060
Yes. It calculates the mathematical badness of every conceivable combination of line breaks from the

09:41.060 --> 09:42.440
first word to the last.

09:43.080 --> 09:47.540
Wait. How does it actually calculate badness, mechanically speaking? Like, what is the math

09:47.540 --> 09:47.820
there?

09:48.020 --> 09:53.540
Well, badness is a mathematically defined metric based on how much the glue in a given line

09:53.540 --> 09:57.080
has to stretch or shrink compared to its natural state.

09:57.240 --> 09:57.500
Okay.

09:57.500 --> 10:02.440
The formula actually cubes the stretch ratio, meaning that a line that is stretched slightly

10:02.440 --> 10:07.440
is fine, but a line stretched heavily becomes exponentially worse in the eyes of the algorithm.

10:07.660 --> 10:09.220
Oh, cubing it makes a huge difference.

10:09.280 --> 10:14.040
It does. The algorithm sums the badness of all the lines, adds the cost to the penalties,

10:14.180 --> 10:19.580
for instance, a penalty for hyphenating two consecutive lines, and computes a total cost

10:19.580 --> 10:21.160
function for the entire paragraph.

10:21.280 --> 10:22.860
And then it just picks the lowest score.

10:22.860 --> 10:28.180
It selects the single path through the paragraph that results in the absolute lowest global badness

10:28.180 --> 10:28.520
score.

10:28.960 --> 10:35.140
That is fascinating. So it's willing to accept a slightly suboptimal, maybe looser first line.

10:35.600 --> 10:41.840
If that specific spacing prevents a catastrophic hyphenation or a massive gap on line four.

10:42.000 --> 10:42.660
Exactly.

10:42.800 --> 10:45.560
It trades local perfection for global optimization.

10:45.980 --> 10:51.160
And that requires evaluating thousands of potential breakpoints in milliseconds. It really

10:51.160 --> 10:53.220
is a masterpiece of computer science.

10:53.380 --> 10:57.400
I understand the elegance of the math there. I really do. But I have to push back on behalf

10:57.400 --> 11:02.720
of anyone who has ever tried to place a complex table or a figure into a latex document.

11:03.000 --> 11:05.620
Ah, floats. The infamous floats.

11:05.760 --> 11:11.560
Yes. Because in practice, it often feels like the engine is actively hostile. You write the code

11:11.560 --> 11:15.420
for a table, you compile the document, and the table randomly jumps three pages down,

11:15.780 --> 11:18.300
landing squarely in the middle of an entirely different section.

11:18.380 --> 11:19.460
Which feels like a bug.

11:19.460 --> 11:24.260
It feels like a massive bug. If the algorithm is so perfectly optimized, why does it exhibit

11:24.260 --> 11:26.100
such chaotic behavior with floats?

11:26.820 --> 11:31.200
The behavior only appears chaotic if you evaluate it through the lens of a word processor.

11:31.600 --> 11:35.100
You are expecting the table to drop exactly where your cursor was.

11:35.440 --> 11:37.260
Because that's what we've been trained to expect.

11:37.400 --> 11:37.620
Right.

11:38.100 --> 11:44.580
But apply the physics we just discussed. To tex, a table is not a special entity. It is simply

11:44.580 --> 11:47.620
a very large, completely rigid box. Oh, I see.

11:47.800 --> 11:53.540
And that massive box is subject to placement rules and penalties. When the engine attempts

11:53.540 --> 11:58.740
to place that box at the exact point in the text where you declared it, it evaluates the

11:58.740 --> 12:01.220
surrounding glue and penalties. And if it doesn't fit?

12:01.220 --> 12:06.740
If placing the table there would cause the text to spill over the bottom margin, or if it would

12:06.740 --> 12:12.580
force a page break that violates a massive penalty, like braking immediately after a section heading,

12:12.580 --> 12:14.020
the engine simply refuses.

12:14.020 --> 12:19.300
It calculates the cost, realizes the badness score would be astronomical, and just aborts

12:19.300 --> 12:20.340
the placement entirely.

12:20.340 --> 12:25.620
Precisely. It holds that rigid box in a holding queue, and waits for the next legal, mathematically

12:25.620 --> 12:28.820
permissible location where the penalties actually allow it to be placed.

12:29.060 --> 12:29.460
Wow.

12:29.860 --> 12:34.740
The table didn't randomly jump. It was sequentially evaluated against the constraints of the page

12:34.740 --> 12:38.820
physics until it found a valid home. It is behaving with absolute determinism.

12:39.180 --> 12:43.920
So it isn't out to get you. It is just strictly enforcing the physical laws you invoked.

12:44.300 --> 12:44.780
Exactly.

12:44.980 --> 12:50.320
And that reframes the frustration completely. But if boxes, glue, and penalties are the

12:50.320 --> 12:55.920
physical matter of the page, how does the engine actually read the raw code we type to generate

12:55.920 --> 13:02.080
that matter? The source material makes a crucial distinction here. It says text does not read words.

13:02.080 --> 13:03.280
It reads tokens.

13:03.280 --> 13:07.680
This is where we cross from typography over into compiler design.

13:07.680 --> 13:08.720
Okay, let's get into it.

13:08.720 --> 13:13.360
To master the system, you must understand the separation between the lexor and the expander.

13:14.000 --> 13:18.240
Before a single box is drawn, the engine has to parse the raw text file you wrote.

13:18.240 --> 13:19.600
Right, the actual code.

13:19.600 --> 13:24.640
But a computer doesn't innately know that a backslash means start a command or that a curly

13:24.640 --> 13:28.800
brace means start a group. It just sees ASCII or UTF-8 characters.

13:28.800 --> 13:30.240
Just a stream of bytes.

13:30.240 --> 13:35.600
Exactly. The lexor's job is to read those raw characters one by one and convert them into

13:35.600 --> 13:41.200
internal tokens. And to do this, it consults a state machine known as the category code table.

13:41.200 --> 13:41.920
The cat codes.

13:41.920 --> 13:42.720
The cat code.

13:42.720 --> 13:46.480
If you spend enough time on stack overflow looking up latex errors,

13:46.480 --> 13:49.200
you eventually run into some wizard talking about cat codes.

13:49.200 --> 13:55.680
It's the secret sauce. Every single character read by the engine is assigned one of 16 category

13:55.680 --> 13:58.800
codes the exact millisecond it is ingested.

13:58.800 --> 13:59.760
Give me an example.

13:59.760 --> 14:05.680
For example, category zero is an escape character, which is mapped by default to the backslash.

14:05.680 --> 14:09.760
Category one is the beginning of a group mapped to the open curly brace.

14:10.400 --> 14:16.480
Category 14 is a comment character mapped to the percent sign. Category 11 is a standard letter.

14:16.480 --> 14:17.200
Okay, that makes sense.

14:17.200 --> 14:21.600
So the lexor takes the raw character, pairs it with its current category code from the table,

14:21.600 --> 14:27.040
and creates an atomic token. From that moment on, the engine only cares about the token,

14:27.040 --> 14:28.000
not the character.

14:28.000 --> 14:32.480
And the architecture allows that category code table to be mutable.

14:32.480 --> 14:35.280
Like you can change the mapping on the fly during compilation.

14:35.280 --> 14:40.560
You can, and understanding that mutability demystifies an enormous amount of the magic

14:40.560 --> 14:41.920
in the latex ecosystem.

14:41.920 --> 14:43.040
What kind of magic?

14:43.040 --> 14:48.160
Consider the verbatim environment, which allows you to print source code in your document

14:48.160 --> 14:51.280
without the latex compiler trying to execute it.

14:51.280 --> 14:54.480
Have you ever considered how the engine accomplishes that mechanically?

14:55.040 --> 14:58.480
Honestly, I always assumed it just suspended the compiler

14:58.480 --> 15:02.960
or flagged the text as a string literal like you would in a Python script.

15:02.960 --> 15:04.960
It is actually far more elegant than that.

15:04.960 --> 15:10.080
The verbatim environment simply issues a command that temporarily changes the category code of almost

15:10.080 --> 15:12.880
every special character to category 12.

15:12.880 --> 15:13.680
What does 12 mean?

15:13.680 --> 15:18.080
Category 12 represents other. So suddenly, when the lexor encounters a backslash,

15:18.080 --> 15:21.520
it does not assign it category 0. It assigns it category 12.

15:21.520 --> 15:24.240
Oh, so it strips the backslash of its power.

15:24.880 --> 15:28.960
Exactly. The backslash is stripped of its power as an escape character.

15:28.960 --> 15:34.720
It is no longer an instruction to start a command. It is merely a printable picture of a backslash.

15:34.720 --> 15:36.960
And the percent sign isn't a comment anymore.

15:36.960 --> 15:40.640
Right. The percent sign is no longer a comment trigger. It's just a symbol.

15:40.640 --> 15:45.840
By manipulating the state machine of the lexor, verbatim neutralizes the code.

15:45.840 --> 15:50.480
That is absolutely brilliant. It literally alters the fundamental laws of physics

15:50.480 --> 15:54.240
for a specific block of text, then reverts them when the block ends.

15:54.240 --> 15:54.800
Yes.

15:54.800 --> 15:56.960
And here's where it gets really interesting for me.

15:57.680 --> 16:02.960
Once the lexor has generated this scream of poke-ins, it passes them to the expander.

16:03.760 --> 16:08.640
When reading this section of the source, I realized that writing latex is not like writing

16:08.640 --> 16:10.480
standard procedural code.

16:10.480 --> 16:11.200
Not at all.

16:11.200 --> 16:16.800
You aren't executing functions that push and pop variables to a call stack in C or JavaScript.

16:16.800 --> 16:21.200
You are engaging in an extreme recursive term rewriting system.

16:21.200 --> 16:26.000
Term rewriting is the precise computer science definition of what text does.

16:26.000 --> 16:28.080
It is a macro expansion language.

16:28.080 --> 16:33.200
So if I define a custom command a macro, I'm not writing a function that returns a value.

16:33.200 --> 16:36.080
I am registering a rewrite rule in the engine's memory.

16:36.080 --> 16:36.720
Correct.

16:36.720 --> 16:41.120
I am telling the expander whenever you encounter this specific sequence of tokens,

16:41.120 --> 16:43.520
physically replace it with this other sequence of tokens.

16:43.520 --> 16:46.240
And this evaluation is inherently recursive.

16:46.240 --> 16:49.840
Once the expander replaces your macro with its defined token sequence,

16:49.840 --> 16:52.320
it immediately examines the new sequence.

16:52.320 --> 16:54.400
Right. To see if there are more macros inside it.

16:54.400 --> 16:55.040
Exactly.

16:55.040 --> 16:58.960
If that new sequence contains further macros, it replaces those as well.

16:58.960 --> 17:04.400
It acts like an abstract syntax tree resolution, continuously expanding the nodes until only

17:04.400 --> 17:10.240
unexpandable primitives remain the literal boxes, glue, fonts, and penalties we discussed earlier.

17:10.240 --> 17:14.880
It just keeps digging down, finding, and replacing until it hits bedrock.

17:14.880 --> 17:16.400
And this expansion is lazy, right?

17:16.400 --> 17:17.200
Extremely lazy.

17:17.200 --> 17:20.800
The engine only expands a token when it is absolutely forced to.

17:20.800 --> 17:21.920
Why is that important?

17:21.920 --> 17:27.680
Because you can pass a complex macro as an argument into another macro, and the engine will not

17:27.680 --> 17:32.320
evaluate the inner macro until the exact moment the outer macro decides to utilize it.

17:32.320 --> 17:33.200
Oh, I see.

17:33.200 --> 17:39.680
This lazy evaluation allows for incredibly complex logic, but it also creates unique debugging

